findings.proposedReproduction:
let mut c = Cache::new(2); c.put("a","1"); c.put("b","2"); // map={a,b}, order=[a,b], at bound
c.put("a","3"); // update, not growth: map.len() 2 >= 2 -> evict_oldest(1) pops "a" (map={b}) then "b" (map={}); then order=["a"], map={a:"3"}
assert_eq!(c.get("b"), Some("2")) FAILS: returns None. Only two distinct keys were ever stored in a 2-entry cache, and the write that caused the eviction was an overwrite requiring no eviction at all. Duplicate-order variant: c2 = Cache::new(3); c2.put("a",..); c2.put("a",..) leaves order=["a","a"] with map.len()==1.
